Description: Restore windows to their places with the session save/restore API of GTK.
 Ptyxis restores its windows with their tabs from its own session state, but a Wayland client
 can neither learn nor set where its windows are, so they open wherever GNOME Shell puts new
 windows. Only the compositor can place them again, by the name the application gives each
 window through the session management protocol. GTK names the windows of an application which
 sets GtkApplication:support-save, saves the names with the rest of its window state, and at
 the next start emits GtkApplication::restore-window for each saved window.
 .
 Ptyxis now sets support-save and gives every window an ID, which it saves both in the window
 state GTK keeps (GtkApplicationWindow::save-state) and with the window in its own session
 state. For each window GTK restores, an empty window is created while GTK emits
 restore-window, as GTK requires, and kept hidden. The session restore then gives it the tabs
 of the window saved under the same ID and shows it, where mutter places it again. A window the
 session restore has no tabs for is destroyed once the command line is handled, and a window
 saved without a known ID is created as before, keeping its ID so that it is placed from the
 next start on. A standalone instance does not set support-save.
 .
 The save/restore API is public only in a GTK with patches/gtk4/, so the property and the
 signals are looked up at runtime: Ptyxis builds against Ubuntu's GTK and behaves as before when
 it runs with it.
Author: Mitar
Forwarded: not-needed
